  "narrative": "Compared to last sale (11-17-26): Yearling steers and heifers were too lightly tested to develop any market trend. Steers and heifer calves were all too lightly tested to develop an accurate market trend, however lower undertones were noticed. Demand was uneven for light offerings. Quality this week was mixed from plain to attractive. Most consignments were offered in small to moderate size packages. Demand was uneven as buyers struggled to build loads with limited offerings. Many cutback cattle continue to be seen this week off summer video sales. CME positions slid lower today with the January and later contracts selling 1.00-2.00 lower. Market activity this week was mostly slow to moderate with a small pool of buyers noticed this week. Weigh up cows sold on mostly light demand for moderate offerings. Feeding cow demand was mostly light moderate this week. Feeding cows sold mostly steady to 3.00 lower on all offerings. Canadian buyers were out of the market this week, which allowed American packers to buy all offerings lower. American packers continue to purchase a few cows to put away for the holidays. Most of these offerings were slightly lame or blemished in some way and purchased mostly at slaughter cow price. Slaughter cows sold mostly steady on breaking cows, boning and lean cows sold mostly 2.00-4.00 lower. Cows offered buyers very good weigh conditions this sale with many very empty offerings noticed. Slaughter bulls sold mostly 3.00 lower in a narrow comparison. Young age cows sold on light to moderate demand this week. Quality was mostly average this week. These offerings sold mostly 10.00-20.00 lower. Offerings over 1100 lbs saw significantly lighter demand this week as most offerings sold near feeding cow price. Supply included: 63% Feeder Cattle (63% Steers, 36% Heifers, 1% Bulls); 23% Slaughter Cattle (80% Cows, 20% Bulls); 14% Replacement Cattle (95% Stock Cows, 5% Open Heifers). Feeder cattle supply over 600 lbs was 24%.",
